				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/eskobar" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1173523); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1173523&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606769002" alt="Eskobar" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1173523)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/eskobar" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1173523); return false;" >Eskobar</a></span> <span class="odate time_1408114877 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">15 Aug 2014 15:01</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2089470">
									

<p>Some very, very weird phrasing throughout. I ordinarily hate nitpicking, but there's a point where the nits are bigger than the rest of the piece.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Nitpicking</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Information related to SCP-2150 is to be blocked from all public service providers.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The woman at Subway who makes me breakfast is a person who provides a public service. Do you mean ISP instead?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the most common resembling a Caucasian male with facial hair.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Me, Burt Reynolds, George R.R. Martin, and Marc Maron are all Caucasian men with facial hair. It's just not a &quot;resemblance&quot; per se. You can say that all or most of the affected by the memetic anomaly <em>are</em> Caucasian men with facial hair, though that begs the question of &quot;why not get elderly female Asian D-class personnel to just do all of the clerical work for this one?&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>despite similar search results being yielded between personnel.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How do you <em>yield something between something</em>?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Instances of SCP-2150-1 express confusion, stating that they have no memories, except for information related to SCP-2150.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What are they confused about, then? They're <em>deluded</em>, certainly, but not <em>confused.</em> They sound pretty sure of themselves to me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Over time, multiple disputes among instances will severely alter any original information gained from themselves.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8230;what? Information gained from themselves? Like, I imagine you have an image in your head of what these Theta-10 events look like, but the image is not conveying itself into my head.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Reintroduction of information to SCP-2150 will overwrite any previous information given from other instances of SCP-2150-1.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>SCP-2150 is the memetic phenomenon associated with the name; how do you introduce information into it? Again, I'm sure this makes sense to you, but I'm not picking up on it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>D-8327M and other infected personnel were detained and administered amnestics before being issued back to Site-███.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>The Researcher was detained and is currently being issued to Site-██.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Issued? You mean, returned?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Revealing more information to you would come so at the risk of infection.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Would come so at? You can just say &quot;would risk infection&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As I am well aware, some O5 members have undergone amnestic treatment prior to promotion, and I am no different.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How could you say a thing <em>without</em> having been well aware of it? The sentence basically comes off as exposition for the audience's benefit&#8230;which is <em>fine</em> in general, as long as it doesn't <em>look</em> like that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>During the writing of SCP-2150, I have received safe information regarding the former Site Director. I have reason to suspect that prior to my promotion, I was previously regarded as this Site Director. My current biological ancestry and criminal records are identical to this man.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Okay, first of all, what the hell even is &quot;safe information&quot; in this case? How does anybody believe anything said about this SCP to be &quot;safe&quot;? For that matter, why is all of this stuff restricted <em>below</em> Level 4 or 5, but <em>the most important and powerful people in the Foundation</em> have free access to uncontained memetic and infohazardous materials?</p>
<p>Also, &quot;<strong>prior</strong> to&#8230; I <strong>was</strong> <strong>previously</strong> regarded as this Site Director&quot; is just &quot;I was the Site Director.&quot; All things &quot;prior&quot; are past tense; all things associated with &quot;was&quot; are past tense; all things that happened &quot;previously&quot; are past tense. You don't need three different words to remind the reader that a thing happened in the not-now-or-in-the-future-time. You can keep the &quot;prior to my promotion&quot; part, but you don't need &quot;was previously&quot; on top of that. &quot;I was the Site Director prior to my promotion&quot; does the same job more economically.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If this is true, then I am <strong>considered</strong> a threat not only to the O5 Council, but also to the Foundation as well.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No, he <em>is</em> a threat. Nobody <em>considers</em> him a threat until they read this and find <em>out</em> that he's a threat. Again, economy of language.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Therefore, I request that I am to be resigned from the O5 Council.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Resign&quot; can be a reflexive verb in some instances (i.e. &quot;I have resigned myself to perdition&quot;), but not a transitive one (i.e. &quot;I resign you to perdition&quot;). He can <em>resign</em>, an act which the O5 Council can then accept or reject implicitly, but that's it. You use the same phrasing again in the next letter.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We deny a request to view documentation, due to risk of exposure.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The sentiment is intelligent, but the request is again malformed. &quot;We will not review the information&quot; can do it, but if the O5s are &quot;denying a request to view documentation&quot;, that usually means they are denying <em>somebody else's</em> request that <em>somebody else</em> be allowed to review documentation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We will keep the original documentation to SCP-2150, so that proper counter measures are enforced when needed be.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Which</em> original documentation? We've referred to something like five different iterations of the documentation. I don't even know which version <em>I</em> read at the beginning of the document. Also, &quot;countermeasures&quot;, &quot;can be enforced&quot; (sort of a future subjunctive mood), and either &quot;when needed&quot; or &quot;when they need to be&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Looking further into his criminal records indicate that the man was convicted countless charges.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Looking&#8230;indicate<strong>s</strong>, convicted <strong>of</strong>, and there is a literal count to the charges that can be ennumerated; your phrasing is just a sort of silly way of making the guy sound super duper bad. You can have six million counts of jaywalking on your record and I'm not gonna be terribly worried about you.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is not certain whether this is true because of the nature of SCP-2150 or the name associated with it. The original documentation of SCP-2150 is now missing; it is likely that the former O5-██ has taken the document with him.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This actually highlights how silly the whole first exchange there was. &quot;I have good reason to suspect that I've secretly been an anomalous pseudo-sleeper agent embedded in the most powerful circle of one of the most powerful groups on the earth. So&#8230;I guess I'm just gonna pack my stuff and head back to the house, right?&quot; &quot;Oh, no, we're vaguely sure that you're not an anomalous pseudo-sleeper agent embedded in the most powerful circle of one of the most powerful groups on the earth, you're just somewhat insane. So yes, you can pack your stuff and head back to the house.&quot; How was this person allowed to just <em>clean out their desk and go home</em>?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is now the Foundation's interest to track down this man, in which some sources refer to him as &quot;The Worst Man in The World&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It is now <strong>in</strong> the Foundation's interests to track down this man, <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">in</span> which some sources refer to <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">him</span> as &quot;The Worst Man in the World. Additionally, that's a really, really weird title for a group of adults to use. It's not &quot;Most Wanted&quot; or &quot;Top Priority;&quot; it's like the name a group of kids who are the Good Guys use to refer to the top Bad Guy on the playground.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It was never intended to have SCP-2150 thrown at us.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That&#8230;is a weird thing to even <em>have</em> to say. To reinforce that the O5 Council dislikes having SCPs used against them. As if someone thought that they were <em>okay</em> with it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>All we know for certain is that hazardous information was presented to us after the promotion of O5-██. Harsher containment procedures on infohazards are the only way we can prevent a situation like this from happening again.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Then every member of the O5 Council is potentially contaminated and needs to be relieved of duty and detained for experimentation. That's the only reasonable conclusion to take from that sentence. Finally, containment procedures need to be <em>more effective</em> and <em>more consistently carried out</em>, not &quot;harsher.&quot;</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>In general, it comes off an SCP tailor-made to make the Foundation look sort of dumb, and the actions of the O5 Council don't help to give off the impression that any of them were ever qualified to run an Exxon station, let alone the Foundation. Downvoted for this conceptual error and the many weird phrasing situations happening throughout.</p>

							</div>

								
					
